{"id":"rm.dynamics-tempo-articulation","slug":"dynamics-tempo-articulation","ladder":"music","course":"rm","unit":"Expression","title":"Dynamics, Tempo and Articulation","courseTitle":"Reading music","unitTitle":"Expression","trouble":"","viz":[],"figureArchetype":"chapter","lesson":{"blocks":[{"t":"chapter","html":"<p class=\"ml-lead\">Most of these marks are Italian words or short forms of them; Italian has been the usual language for musical directions for centuries. You only need a handful to read most music.</p>\n\n<h2>Dynamics: loud and soft</h2>\n<p>Dynamics are written below the staff (between the staves in piano music), in bold italic letters:</p>\n<div class=\"ml-tw\"><table class=\"ml-table\">\n  <thead><tr><th>Sign</th><th>Italian</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">pp</td><td>pianissimo</td><td>very soft</td></tr>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">p</td><td>piano</td><td>soft</td></tr>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">mp</td><td>mezzo piano</td><td>medium soft</td></tr>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">mf</td><td>mezzo forte</td><td>medium loud</td></tr>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">f</td><td>forte</td><td>loud</td></tr>\n    <tr><td class=\"ml-sym\">ff</td><td>fortissimo</td><td>very loud</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The piano’s full name, <i>pianoforte</i>, means “soft-loud,” because it could play both, depending on how you press the keys.</p>\n<figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src='{\"rh\": \"C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 C5/4 | C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 C5/4\", \"marks\": {\"1\": {\"dyn\": \"p\"}, \"2\": {\"dyn\": \"f\"}}}' data-tempo=\"100\" data-max=\"560\" data-label=\"The same four notes twice: first marked p, soft; then f, loud\">\n  <figcaption>The same notes, first <i>p</i>, then <i>f</i>. Press <i>Hear it</i>.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>Gradual changes are written as words or as long, thin wedges called hairpins. <b>Crescendo</b> (<i>cresc.</i>, or a wedge opening to the right) means get louder little by little. <b>Diminuendo</b> or <b>decrescendo</b> (<i>dim.</i>, <i>decresc.</i>, or a wedge closing to the right) means get softer.</p>\n<figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src='{\"rh\": \"C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 F5/4 | G5/4 A5/4 B5/4 C6/4 | C6/1\", \"marks\": {\"1\": {\"dyn\": [\"p\", \"cresc.@2\"]}, \"3\": {\"dyn\": \"f\"}}}' data-vels=\"0.3 0.36 0.42 0.48 0.55 0.62 0.69 0.76 0.82\" data-tempo=\"100\" data-max=\"640\" data-label=\"A rising line marked p, then cresc., arriving at f\">\n  <figcaption>Starting soft, a crescendo grows until it reaches <i>f</i>.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2>Tempo: fast and slow</h2>\n<p>A word above the first bar sets the speed. These are the most common, from slow to fast. The beats per minute are only rough guides; musicians and editions differ.</p>\n<div class=\"ml-tw\"><table class=\"ml-table\">\n  <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Roughly (beats per minute)</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><b>Largo</b></td><td>broad, very slow</td><td>40–60</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Adagio</b></td><td>slow, at ease</td><td>66–76</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Andante</b></td><td>at a walking pace</td><td>76–108</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Moderato</b></td><td>moderate</td><td>108–120</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Allegro</b></td><td>quick and bright</td><td>120–156</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><b>Presto</b></td><td>very fast</td><td>168–200</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Many pieces also print a metronome mark such as ♩ = 100: one hundred quarter-note beats per minute. The <a href=\"../../metronome/\">metronome</a> shows the tempo word for any speed you choose.</p>\n<div class=\"ml-cols\">\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src='{\"rh\": \"E4/4 E4/4 F4/4 G4/4 | G4/4 F4/4 E4/4 D4/4\", \"marks\": {\"1\": {\"tempo\": \"Adagio\"}}}' data-tempo=\"66\" data-label=\"The start of Ode to Joy marked Adagio\">\n    <figcaption><i>Adagio</i>: slow.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src='{\"rh\": \"E4/4 E4/4 F4/4 G4/4 | G4/4 F4/4 E4/4 D4/4\", \"marks\": {\"1\": {\"tempo\": \"Allegro\"}}}' data-tempo=\"138\" data-label=\"The start of Ode to Joy marked Allegro\">\n    <figcaption><i>Allegro</i>: quick and bright.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n</div>\n<p>Changes of speed: <b>ritardando</b> (<i>rit.</i>) or <b>rallentando</b> (<i>rall.</i>) means gradually slower; <b>accelerando</b> (<i>accel.</i>) gradually faster; <b>a tempo</b> means go back to the speed you had before. A <b>fermata</b> (a curve with a dot under it) over a note means hold it longer than its written value, for as long as feels right.</p>\n<figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src='{\"rh\": \"G4/4 E4/4 F4/4 D4/4 | C4/1\", \"marks\": {\"1\": {\"above\": \"rit.\"}, \"2\": {\"fermata\": true}}}' data-hear=\"0\" data-max=\"440\" data-label=\"Two bars: rit. above the first bar, and a fermata over the final whole note\">\n  <figcaption>Slow down through the first bar (<i>rit.</i>), then hold the last note (the fermata).</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2>Articulation: how each note is played</h2>\n<div class=\"ml-cols ml-two\">\n  <div class=\"ml-card\"><h3>Legato</h3><p>Smooth and connected, each note flowing into the next. A curved line over or under different notes, a <b>slur</b>, asks for legato.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"ml-card\"><h3>Staccato</h3><p>A dot above or below the note: make it short and detached, with a little silence after it.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"ml-card\"><h3>Accent</h3><p>A sideways wedge &gt;: play that note with more weight, louder than the notes around it.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"ml-card\"><h3>Tenuto</h3><p>A short line: hold the note for its full value, often leaning on it slightly.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ml-cols ml-two\">\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src=\"(C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 F5/4) | G5/1\" data-time=\"4/4\" data-tempo=\"96\" data-label=\"Four notes under a slur, played legato\">\n    <figcaption>Legato, under a slur.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src=\"C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 F5/4 | G5/1\" data-artic=\". . . . _\" data-time=\"4/4\" data-tempo=\"96\" data-label=\"The same notes with staccato dots, played short\">\n    <figcaption>Staccato: the same notes, short and crisp.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src=\"C5/4 C5/4 C5/4 C5/4 | C5/4 C5/4 C5/4 C5/4\" data-artic=\"> _ _ _ > _ _ _\" data-vels=\"0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.5\" data-time=\"4/4\" data-tempo=\"96\" data-label=\"Repeated notes with an accent on every first beat\">\n    <figcaption>Accents on beat 1 of each bar.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n  <figure data-ml=\"staff\" data-src=\"E5/4 E5/4 E5/4 E5/4 | D5/1\" data-artic=\"- - - - _\" data-time=\"4/4\" data-tempo=\"96\" data-label=\"Notes with tenuto lines, each held its full length\">\n    <figcaption>Tenuto: full length, slightly leaned on.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n</div>\n\n<div data-ml=\"drill\" data-type=\"quiz\" data-id=\"rm-expression-quiz\" data-title=\"What does it mean?\">\n<script type=\"application/json\">[\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"pp\", \"choices\": [\"very soft\", \"very loud\", \"medium soft\", \"gradually louder\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"pp is pianissimo, very soft.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"mf\", \"choices\": [\"medium loud\", \"medium soft\", \"very loud\", \"gradually softer\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"mf is mezzo forte, medium loud.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"f\", \"choices\": [\"loud\", \"soft\", \"fast\", \"hold the note\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"f is forte, loud.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"cresc.\", \"choices\": [\"gradually louder\", \"gradually softer\", \"gradually slower\", \"very loud\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Crescendo: grow louder little by little.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"dim.\", \"choices\": [\"gradually softer\", \"gradually louder\", \"gradually faster\", \"short and detached\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Diminuendo: get softer little by little.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"How do you play these notes?\", \"staff\": \"C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 F5/4\", \"o\": {\"artic\": \". . . .\"}, \"choices\": [\"short and detached\", \"smooth and connected\", \"loud\", \"held extra long\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Dots above or below the notes mean staccato.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"How do you play the first note?\", \"staff\": \"G4/4 G4/4 G4/4 G4/4\", \"o\": {\"artic\": \"> _ _ _\"}, \"choices\": [\"with extra weight, louder\", \"very short\", \"softer than the others\", \"twice as long\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"The wedge is an accent.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"How do you play these notes?\", \"staff\": \"(C5/4 D5/4 E5/4 F5/4)\", \"choices\": [\"smooth and connected\", \"short and detached\", \"all at once\", \"gradually faster\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"A slur over different notes means legato: smooth and connected.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does the sign over the last note mean?\", \"staff\": {\"rh\": \"E4/2 D4/2 | C4/1\", \"marks\": {\"2\": {\"fermata\": true}}}, \"o\": {\"timeSig\": true}, \"hear\": false, \"choices\": [\"hold it longer than written\", \"play it very short\", \"repeat it\", \"play it louder\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"That’s a fermata: hold the note as long as feels right.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this tempo word mean?\", \"big\": \"Allegro\", \"choices\": [\"quick and bright\", \"slow\", \"at a walking pace\", \"very soft\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Allegro is quick and bright.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this tempo word mean?\", \"big\": \"Andante\", \"choices\": [\"at a walking pace\", \"very fast\", \"very slow\", \"loud\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Andante means at a walking pace.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this tempo word mean?\", \"big\": \"Adagio\", \"choices\": [\"slow\", \"fast\", \"moderate\", \"gradually faster\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Adagio is slow, at ease.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"What does this mark mean?\", \"big\": \"rit.\", \"choices\": [\"gradually slower\", \"gradually softer\", \"return to the beginning\", \"gradually faster\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"Ritardando: slow down little by little.\"},\n  {\"q\": \"After a rit., what does a tempo tell you?\", \"big\": \"a tempo\", \"choices\": [\"go back to the earlier speed\", \"play twice as fast\", \"stop\", \"play softly\"], \"a\": 0, \"why\": \"A tempo means return to the tempo you had before the change.\"}\n]</script>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"ml-sum\">\n  <h2>Remember</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Dynamics: pp, p, mp, mf, f, ff; cresc. louder, dim. softer.</li>\n    <li>Tempo words set the speed: Largo, Adagio, Andante, Moderato, Allegro, Presto. rit. slows, a tempo returns.</li>\n    <li>Legato (slur) smooth; staccato (dot) short; accent (&gt;) stronger; tenuto (line) full length; fermata: hold.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>","meta":{"pill":"Expression","lead":"The notes say what to play. These marks say how: loud or soft, fast or slow, smooth or crisp.","icon":"🎻"}}],"sources":[{"name":"Teddy Rook Book — Music"}]},"practice":null}